## 6. Run, diagnose, and repair the problematic join

<p class="ryg-part"><span class="ryg-label ryg-yellow">YELLOW</span> AI may assist with join, duplicate-removal, or debugging syntax; verify all pre/post row counts and the repaired key.</p>
<p class="ryg-part"><span class="ryg-label ryg-red">RED</span> Identify the specific failure and explain why the repair is valid using your own reasoning.</p>

Run the initial join and compare pre-/post-join rows. Identify the specific key that creates accidental duplication.

**DEU / 2020 appears twice in the unemployment chart so it is there duplicated**

```{r}
# Initial join, row comparison, and diagnosis here.
combined_data <- gdp_long |>
  left_join(unemployment, by = c("country_code", "year"))
View(combined_data)
```

The duplicated unemployment record is exact. Create `unemployment_clean` with one copy of each exact economy-year record. Recheck the key and rerun the left join as `gdp_unemployment`.

```{r}
# Repair and clean join here.
unemployment_clean <- unemployment |>
  distinct(country_code, year, .keep_all = TRUE)
View(unemployment_clean)

gdp_unemployment <- gdp_long |>
  left_join(unemployment_clean, by = c("country_code", "year"))
View(gdp_unemployment)
```

