### Block A — Customers and service requests

**Customers**

| customer_id | region |
|---|---|
| C01 | North |
| C02 | South |
| C03 | West |

**Service requests**

| request_id | customer_id | issue |
|---|---|---|
| R1 | C01 | billing |
| R2 | C01 | delivery |
| R3 | C03 | billing |
| R4 | C99 | delivery |

<p class="ryg-part"><span class="ryg-label ryg-red">RED</span> Without R or AI: (a) state the unit of observation and a valid key for each table; (b) describe the relationship from customers to requests among matching IDs; (c) predict the number of rows in a customers-left join and explain each contribution; (d) predict the number of rows in an inner join; and (e) identify the unmatched record(s) omitted or retained by each join.</p>

**In both charts the matching key is the customer id, and in the first chart we are observing region vs the second one observing issues. Each request id is matched to a different customer from some region, and the request is for a certain issue. In a customers left-join, all customers rows would stay, but the c99 row in service requests would not join with it because there's no c99 in the "left" chart, so there would be 4 rows (c01,c01,c02,c03 and both c01s would have north region). An inner join keeps rows from both charts that have a match, so this would be both c01 requests matched with the c01 north region (2 rows) and the c03 in both (1 row) for a total of 3 rows, leaving out c02 and c99.**

